    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n101",
        "text": "101. Faraday's laws\n\nFaraday summarized his investigations of electrolysis in two laws.\nThey relate the electric charge passing through an electrolyte to the mass of substance deposited at an electrode.\n\n**Faraday's first law**\nFor the same deposited substance and electrode reaction conditions, suppose the current is constant and side reactions can be neglected.\n\n**During time $t$, the mass $m$ deposited at an electrode is proportional to the duration $t$ and to the current $I$**:\n$m=kIt=kq$.\n(10-5)\n\nHere $k$ is the proportionality coefficient, and $It=q$ is the charge passing through the electrolyte during time $t$.\nAccording to formula (10-5), the numerical value of $k$ gives the mass deposited when 1 coulomb passes.\n\n**The coefficient $k$ is called the electrochemical equivalent of the substance**.\n\nIf other reactions consume some charge, distinguish the total charge passed from the charge actually used in the intended deposition reaction.\n\nFormula (10-5) shows that the SI unit of the electrochemical equivalent $k$ is kilogram per coulomb (kg/C).\nIt is the unit of a mass-to-charge ratio.\n\nWhen the deposition reaction is specified and other reactions have negligible effects, measuring the deposited mass $m$ and the corresponding charge $q$ determines the substance's electrochemical equivalent.\n\nIonic conduction and electrode reactions help explain Faraday's first law.\nFor the same deposition reaction, more ions participating in charge transfer correspond to more charge passed and a greater deposited mass.\nThe proportionality assumes the same reaction conditions.\n\nFigure 194 shows an arrangement for testing Faraday's first law.\nThree electrolytic cells contain the same electrolyte.\n\nCompare deposits produced by the same electrode reaction during the same time interval.\nThe currents through the cells differ and are denoted by $I_1$, $I_2$, and $I_3$.\n\nThe current division in the arrangement gives:\n$I_1=I_2+I_3$.\nMeasure the deposited masses $m_1$, $m_2$, and $m_3$ and test whether they are proportional to the currents $I_1$, $I_2$, and $I_3$.\n\nKeep each current constant during the comparison and avoid differences in side reactions that would affect the deposits.\n\n**Faraday's second law**\nThe electrochemical equivalent depends on a substance's mass characteristics and on the valence involved in its electrode reaction.\n\nFor example, K and Br correspond here to singly charged ions.\nKBr dissociates in solution into $K^+$ and $Br^-$.\nTheir charges have the same magnitude as an electron's charge, but opposite signs.\n\nDissolved copper sulfate, $\\mathrm{CuSO}_4$, dissociates into $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{SO}_4^{2-}$.\nEach ion's charge magnitude is twice the electron charge magnitude, and copper is divalent in this compound.\n\nThis describes the charges carried by the ions.\nIt does not mean that dissociation consists of bare copper atoms spontaneously losing electrons.\n\nFigure 194\n\nLet $n$ denote the valence appropriate to the ion and electrode reaction under discussion.\nLet $A$ denote what the original textbook calls the \"atomic weight.\"\n\nWith the SI units used in this lesson, this quantity must mean the corresponding molar mass, not a dimensionless relative atomic mass.**\nThe ratio $\\frac{A}{n}$ is called the chemical equivalent**, with unit kilogram per mole (kg/mol).\n\nIt is a mass-per-mole quantity adjusted for valence, not a particle count.\n\nThe three electrolytic cells in Figure 195 contain different electrolytes and are connected in series.\nThe current is the same throughout the series circuit.\n\nDuring the same time $t$, the charge passing through each cell is therefore the same.\nFor constant current:\n$q=It$.\n\nWhen the electrode reactions are specified and side reactions can be neglected, the deposited masses are proportional to their respective chemical equivalents.\nThis comparison uses the same charge passed.\nIt does not mean that different substances must deposit equal masses.\n\nFigure 195\n\nFaraday's first law, formula (10-5), says that for the same charge $q$, the deposited mass $m$ is proportional to the electrochemical equivalent $k$.\nCombining this with the comparison of different electrolytes shows that **a substance's electrochemical equivalent is proportional to its chemical equivalent $\\frac{A}{n}$**.\nThis is Faraday's second law.\n\nThe valence must correspond to the electrode reaction that actually occurs.\n\nUse $\\frac{1}{F}$ for the proportionality coefficient relating the chemical equivalent $\\frac{A}{n}$ to the electrochemical equivalent $k$.\nFaraday's second law becomes:\n\n$k=\\frac{1}{F}\\frac{A}{n}$.\n(10-6)\n\nThe quantity $F$ is called the **Faraday constant**.\nIt is not a material parameter that varies arbitrarily with the kind of electrolyte.\n\n**Physical meaning of the Faraday constant**\nSubstitute expression (10-6) for $k$ into formula (10-5):\n\n$m=\\frac{1}{F}\\cdot\\frac{A}{n}\\cdot q$.\n(10-7)\n\nThis relates the deposited mass, chemical equivalent, and charge passed.\n\nThe Faraday constant is the charge corresponding to the transfer of one mole of electrons.\nThe textbook explains this using numerical values in specified units.\nIf the mass in kilograms has the same numerical value as the chemical equivalent in kilograms per mole, the required charge in coulombs has the same numerical value as the Faraday constant in coulombs per mole.\n\nThis compares numerical values in specified units.\nMass and molar mass are not the same physical quantity.\n\nThe chemical equivalent $\\frac{A}{n}$ is a mass-per-mole quantity adjusted for valence.\nThe textbook uses $\\frac{N_A}{n}$ as the correspondingly adjusted particle-number coefficient, where $N_A$ is the Avogadro constant.\n\nIts unit is per mole.\nMultiplying it by the magnitude of charge transferred by one corresponding ion gives the Faraday constant:\n\n$F=q_n\\cdot\\frac{N_A}{n}$.\n(10-8)\n\nHere $q_n$ is the magnitude of the charge transferred by one corresponding ion.\nIt is not a signed positive or negative ionic charge.\nThe valence must correspond to the ion and electrode reaction under discussion.\n\nThe textbook uses the experimental approximation $F=96500$ coulombs per mole.\nThis rounded value is used for its calculations.\nIt is not an exact value in the modern unit system.\n\n**Determining the magnitude of the electron charge**\nThe Faraday constant, ionic valence, and particle count can be combined to determine the electron charge magnitude.\n\nFor copper sulfate solution ($\\mathrm{CuSO}_4$), the copper ion has valence 2, so $n=2$.\nThe chemical equivalent $\\frac{A}{n}$ of copper is 0.03177 kilograms per mole.\n\nFor a copper deposit whose mass in kilograms has that same numerical value, the textbook uses a charge of 96500 coulombs.\nUsing its approximate values, the particle-number coefficient adjusted for valence is:\n$\\frac{N_A}{n}=\\frac{6.02\\times10^{23}}{2}$ /mol $=3.01\\times10^{23}$ /mol.\n\nThe unit /mol belongs to the particle-number coefficient.\nIt is not a unit attached to the actual number of particles in a particular deposit.\n\nDividing the Faraday constant by this coefficient gives the charge magnitude corresponding to one copper ion $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$:\n$q_n=\\frac{F}{\\frac{N_A}{2}}=\\frac{96500}{3.01\\times10^{23}}$ coulombs $\\approx3.2\\times10^{-19}$ coulombs.\n(10-9)\n\nThe result's precision must be consistent with the approximate data used.\n\nWhen a copper ion becomes a neutral copper atom at the electrode, it gains two electrons.\nThe magnitude of one electron's charge is therefore:\n$e=\\frac{q_n}{2}=1.6\\times10^{-19}$ coulombs $=4.8\\times10^{-10}$ electrostatic charge units.\n\nThese are the textbook's approximate values and its conversion to an older electrostatic unit.\nA positive charge magnitude does not mean that the electron has positive charge.\n\nThe textbook compares the electron charge magnitude obtained by this method with results from other methods and reports agreement at the precision it uses.\nIt mentions experiments by Joffe and Millikan and refers to the seventh-grade physics textbook for background.\nThese methods start from different experiments and test the same charge magnitude.\n\nThe use of electrolysis data to determine the electron charge magnitude came many years after Faraday proposed his electrolysis laws.\nAt that earlier time, the internal structure of atoms and molecules was not understood, and electrons were not yet known.\nThe electrolysis laws were experimental regularities before they received a microscopic explanation.\n\nExperimental studies of electrolysis help explain conduction and electrode reactions in aqueous electrolyte solutions and molten electrolytes.\nIon migration carries charge through the solution or melt.\n\nCharge transfer at the electrodes is related to the deposited mass.\nDistinguish these two parts of the process."
      }
